In the worksheet, explain what .gitignore does, why the two file patterns you added matter, and what information the commit history reveals about a project's development over time.
The `.gitignore` file is used to tell Git which files and folders should not be tracked or included in commits. In this project, I added patterns to ignore Python cache folders, `.pyc` compiled files, and `.env` files. Ignoring cache folders and `.pyc` files is helpful because Python creates them automatically, and they are not necessary to share with other developers. The `.env` file is particularly important because it may contain sensitive information such as passwords, API keys, and other private configuration details that should not be uploaded to a Git repository.
The commit history shows how the project has developed over time. It keeps a record of important details such as the commit message, the person who made the change, the date and time, and the unique commit ID. By reviewing the commit history, developers can easily understand what changes were made, when they were made, and how the project has progressed. It also makes it easier to identify problems, track previous changes, and restore the project to an earlier version when necessary.







